   "why": [
    {
     "rank": 1,
     "kind": "authored",
     "text": "Temperature sets the water’s density, and so the layering that decides whether deep nutrients reach the sunlit surface; off California the warm years are El Niño years and marine heatwaves.",
     "cites": [
      {
       "key": "bond2015",
       "label": "Bond et al. 2015",
       "url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/2015GL063306"
      }
     ],
     "bibkeys": [
      "bond2015"
     ],
     "eov": "Subsurface temperature",
     "goos_doc": "https://goosocean.org/document/17467"
    },
    {
     "rank": 2,
     "kind": "authored",
     "text": "A warm year off California is usually a lean one: warmer, more layered water brings fewer nutrients up, and the plankton the rest of the food web eats decline.",
     "cites": [
      {
       "key": "roemmich1995",
       "label": "Roemmich & McGowan 1995",
